 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Perth's electricity prices are rising more slowly than in most of the country, so the data doesn't show Perth as an outlier.** CPI tracks prices, not bill totals, so it can't tell you whether your own bill is unusual.\n\n**Electricity prices, year to August 2026 (ABS CPI)**\n- Perth: **+2.4%**. It was +2.5% in each month from April to June 2026, so tariff rises have been small and steady.\n- Australia (eight-capital average): **+13.2%**. It peaked at +37% in February 2026.\n- Perth all-items CPI: **+4.3%**. Perth electricity is rising more slowly than prices overall.\n\n**Why the Perth figures swing so much**\nPerth's monthly electricity changes jump around, for example +289% in August 2025 and −50% in January 2025. This comes from government energy rebates and credits that cut the measured price in some months and then dropped out. On the index, with September 2025 = 100, Perth sat at about 26 in late 2024 and early 2025, 100 in mid-2025, about 69 in November and December 2025, and 102.4 in August 2026. So some of the \"huge bill\" feeling could come from credits ending, if you received them.\n\n**Why your bill might still feel big**\n- **Usage:** winter heating or summer air-conditioning can raise a bill even when the price per unit barely moves.\n- **Other costs:** Perth's overall inflation is 4.3%, and that includes your power bill. Everything else may be adding to the sense of squeeze.\n- **Base effect:** prices were very low in 2024 and early 2025 because of the rebates, so today's bill looks large against that.\n\nIf you tell me roughly what your bill was a year ago and what it is now, I can help work out whether the change is price, usage or lost credits.",
